JOHN WILKES: I'm good.

00:04:07.836 --> 00:04:10.222
I'm delightfully grateful
for having me on.

00:04:10.222 --> 00:04:11.180
MARK MANDEL: Wonderful.

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:13.062
Thank you so much
for joining us.

00:04:13.062 --> 00:04:14.520
For those people
who don't know who

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:17.160
you are, whoever
those people may be--

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:19.019
I've been following
your stuff for ages

00:04:19.019 --> 00:04:20.640
and been a huge fan of
yours for a very long time.

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:22.098
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Mark is incredibly

00:04:22.098 --> 00:04:23.680
excited about this episode.

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:24.914
MARK MANDEL: I am.

00:04:24.914 --> 00:04:27.080
Please, can you tell us a
little bit about yourself,

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:28.420
what do you do here at Google?

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:31.030
JOHN WILKES: I'm just this guy.

00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:32.490
I work in the Borg
team, which is

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:34.340
part of our technical
infrastructure folk.

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:37.320
We write software that runs
a fleet of machines that

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:39.899
runs across-- pretty much
every other stuff that

00:04:39.899 --> 00:04:41.440
runs inside Google
runs on top of us,

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:43.790
so, from the outside world,
Google Compute Engine,

00:04:43.790 --> 00:04:46.930
and pretty much all the cloud
products run on top of Borg.

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:48.560
Borg runs on top
a set of machines.

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:50.272
Borg allocates those
machines on demand

00:04:50.272 --> 00:04:51.980
when people come along
and say, you know,

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:53.950
I need 4,000 places to run this.

00:04:53.950 --> 00:04:55.920
Could you find
those 4,000 places?

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:56.850
We'll pick them.

00:04:56.850 --> 00:04:58.650
We will cope with the fact
that three of them will break.

00:04:58.650 --> 00:04:59.710
We'll put them somewhere else.

00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:01.640
We'll work out how else
we can patch things

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:03.860
into these clusters of
machines to make them used

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:06.250
as efficiently as possible.

00:05:06.250 --> 00:05:08.810
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So one
question that-- I've

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:11.050
been working at Google
for quite a while,

00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:13.500
but I'm actually not
sure about the answer.

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:15.690
For how long we've had Borg?

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:17.460
Did we create it from
the beginning, or?

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:20.400
JOHN WILKES: We started it
around about 2003, 2004,

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:22.220
long before I
joined the company.

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:25.250
So it began as a sort of
small-scale activity with about

00:05:25.250 --> 00:05:27.940
three or four people
in collaboration

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:30.240
with a new version
of our search engine.

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:32.470
So they grew up together.

00:05:32.470 --> 00:05:34.580
Each built on little
features of the other,

00:05:34.580 --> 00:05:37.430
and [INAUDIBLE] that was used
as initial deployment partner.

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:39.920
And from then, people realized
it was just really helpful

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:41.030
for doing a bunch
of stuff, so they

00:05:41.030 --> 00:05:43.654
could focus on what it was they
were trying to do in the search

00:05:43.654 --> 00:05:45.370
tools, and the
underlying system would

00:05:45.370 --> 00:05:46.630
cope with a bunch of
stuff that they didn't

00:05:46.630 --> 00:05:48.040
want to have to deal with.

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:49.670
And that was useful not
only for the search team,

00:05:49.670 --> 00:05:50.610
but for other folk as well.

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:52.440
So it's started getting
widespread adoption.

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:54.648
MARK MANDEL: And credit
where credit's due, your name

00:05:54.648 --> 00:05:55.856
is on the Borg paper.

00:05:55.856 --> 00:05:57.230
JOHN WILKES: So
I wrote the paper

00:05:57.230 --> 00:05:58.530
with a bunch of
other collaborators

00:05:58.530 --> 00:06:00.321
who did a bunch of work
and experimentation

00:06:00.321 --> 00:06:03.390
about the system that many,
many other people had built.

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:05.827
So I'm one of a
huge team of folk

00:06:05.827 --> 00:06:07.080
who've done the actual work.

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:09.620
I'm just a guy who ends up being
the poor person talking out

00:06:09.620 --> 00:06:11.030
in the front.

00:06:11.030 --> 00:06:13.530
But the people who constructed
the system, they run it,

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:15.210
they keep on developing
it right now.

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:15.690
MARK MANDEL: That's cool.

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:17.350
So I guess that
means that if you're

00:06:17.350 --> 00:06:21.050
someone who's used to
Maps, or Search, or Gmail,

00:06:21.050 --> 00:06:23.720
anything even above JCP
in Google cloud platform,

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:24.575
you've used Borg.

00:06:24.575 --> 00:06:25.450
JOHN WILKES: Exactly.

00:06:25.450 --> 00:06:27.465
With luck, you haven't known it.

00:06:27.465 --> 00:06:28.590
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very nice.

00:06:28.590 --> 00:06:32.950
So for the people that have
never heard about Borg,

00:06:32.950 --> 00:06:34.390
how would you describe it?

00:06:34.390 --> 00:06:36.882
What does it do?

00:06:36.882 --> 00:06:38.340
JOHN WILKES: It
basically means you

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:41.010
don't have to worry about which
machines you're running on.

00:06:41.010 --> 00:06:44.040
So you write-- let's say
you're a Gmail developer.

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:49.120
So you're interested in a next
version of the Gmail service.

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:50.340
So you build a version that.

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:51.080
You try it out.

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:52.910
Typically, you can
test it in our world,

00:06:52.910 --> 00:06:56.690
either on your local machine,
or in some Cloud test systems,

00:06:56.690 --> 00:06:59.497
and then you just say, great,
let's run that on Borg.

00:06:59.497 --> 00:07:00.080
And that's it.

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:02.140
And you say, can
I have 400 copies?

00:07:02.140 --> 00:07:03.082
They say, sure.

00:07:03.082 --> 00:07:05.040
And that's all you have
to think about, really,

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:07.180
provided you run and jump
through a few other hoops

00:07:07.180 --> 00:07:08.480
we'll get to in a minute.

00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:09.740
But you don't have
to worry about where

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:10.640
those machines come from.

00:07:10.640 --> 00:07:12.890
You don't have to worry
about what other things are

00:07:12.890 --> 00:07:13.900
running on the same machine.

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:16.150
You don't have to worry about
if your task falls over,

00:07:16.150 --> 00:07:17.820
and you need to be restarted.

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:20.226
All of that stuff just
gets taken care of for you.

00:07:20.226 --> 00:07:22.225
It ties in with things
like the security system.

00:07:22.225 --> 00:07:23.695
It ties in with
the storage system.

00:07:23.695 --> 00:07:25.910
So it gives you access
to data elsewhere.

00:07:25.910 --> 00:07:28.170
It ties in with our internal
network and communication

00:07:28.170 --> 00:07:29.000
system.

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:31.430
So you can communicate
it inside the company

00:07:31.430 --> 00:07:33.296
and also through
the outside world

00:07:33.296 --> 00:07:35.670
in a secure fashion in a way
that everything is encrypted

00:07:35.670 --> 00:07:37.540
pretty much in flight.

00:07:37.540 --> 00:07:40.280
And it just relieves a bunch
of pressure and tension

00:07:40.280 --> 00:07:42.390
from the developers
so they can focus

00:07:42.390 --> 00:07:44.900
on adding the business value
that they're interested in.

00:07:44.900 --> 00:07:46.490
MARK MANDEL: So you're trying
to tell me that people at Google

00:07:46.490 --> 00:07:48.330
aren't just, like,
SSH-ing into machines

00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:49.780
and just copying
binaries across?

00:07:49.780 --> 00:07:50.080
JOHN WILKES: Pretty much.

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:51.807
One of the things
that Borg helps with

00:07:51.807 --> 00:07:53.390
is to make sure that
the only time you

00:07:53.390 --> 00:07:57.070
can SSH into a machine, you have
to go through a binary, which

00:07:57.070 --> 00:07:59.612
we provide that makes sure
that you have a reason

00:07:59.612 --> 00:08:02.070
to be on that machine, that
there is some part of something

00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:03.836
that you have authority
to access that

00:08:03.836 --> 00:08:04.960
is running on that machine.

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:06.790
Otherwise, the answer is nope.

00:08:06.790 --> 00:08:09.550
MARK MANDEL: Nope. yeah.

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:11.120
No, that's very cool.

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:14.112
Having looked at-- and I'll
reference this probably a lot,

00:08:14.112 --> 00:08:15.570
there was a particular
presentation

00:08:15.570 --> 00:08:17.760
you gave at dotScale,
where you wrote,

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:19.400
like, a declarative file.

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:22.310
And you were showing how to
do "hello world" at Scale

00:08:22.310 --> 00:08:25.052
with something like 10,000
copies or something ridiculous.

00:08:25.052 --> 00:08:27.385
What sort of stuff would you
end up putting inside that?

00:08:27.385 --> 00:08:28.968
So I'm guessing you
had, like, a name,

00:08:28.968 --> 00:08:31.510
and there's a binary
you want to run?

00:08:31.510 --> 00:08:32.390
Something like that?

00:08:32.390 --> 00:08:32.809
JOHN WILKES: Pretty much.

00:08:32.809 --> 00:08:34.539
So imagine you've
compiled "Hello world"

00:08:34.539 --> 00:08:36.052
to run on your local desktop.

00:08:36.052 --> 00:08:37.760
So you bring it up to
make sure it works,

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:40.780
and it displays "Hello,
world" in an exciting fashion

00:08:40.780 --> 00:08:42.179
in a local screen.

00:08:42.179 --> 00:08:45.330
And then you wrap it
with a description

00:08:45.330 --> 00:08:46.900
about what you would
like to be true

00:08:46.900 --> 00:08:48.190
when it is running on Borg.

00:08:48.190 --> 00:08:49.880
And I use those terms carefully.

00:08:49.880 --> 00:08:50.885
So you say, first
of all, I've got

00:08:50.885 --> 00:08:52.176
to give it a name of some kind.

00:08:52.176 --> 00:08:55.990
So we call a collection of tasks
running the same thing, a job.

00:08:55.990 --> 00:08:59.240
So a job has a name, [INAUDIBLE]
texturing, and username,

00:08:59.240 --> 00:09:00.250
and things like that.

00:09:00.250 --> 00:09:01.870
You say where you
want to run it.

00:09:01.870 --> 00:09:05.120
And typically you say a cell
which, for us, is median size

00:09:05.120 --> 00:09:06.684
cell is about 10,000 machines.

00:09:06.684 --> 00:09:08.600
We have them scattered
pretty much all the way

00:09:08.600 --> 00:09:09.370
across the globe.

00:09:09.370 --> 00:09:11.336
If you go to your
favorite search engine,

00:09:11.336 --> 00:09:13.960
you'll be able to find a map of
where Google data centers live.

00:09:13.960 --> 00:09:16.659
Each of those runs,
typically, several cells.

00:09:16.659 --> 00:09:18.450
And you get to pick
one for various reasons

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:21.416
to do with allocation
policies on the next level up.

00:09:21.416 --> 00:09:23.147
You choose, you say,
I want to be there.

00:09:23.147 --> 00:09:25.480
You say, here's the program
I've written, "Hello, world"

00:09:25.480 --> 00:09:26.021
in this case.

00:09:26.021 --> 00:09:28.440
You provide a path
to access that.

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:30.030
If you want to,
you can say things

00:09:30.030 --> 00:09:32.810
like how many CPU
calls it might need,

00:09:32.810 --> 00:09:35.230
or how many resources it
should consume in terms of RAM,

00:09:35.230 --> 00:09:36.470
disk space, things like that.

00:09:36.470 --> 00:09:38.485
Or you can say,
ah, choose for me.

00:09:38.485 --> 00:09:40.110
Let the system do
that, because we have

00:09:40.110 --> 00:09:41.660
a technique for doing that.

00:09:41.660 --> 00:09:44.740
And then you say how
many copies you want.

00:09:44.740 --> 00:09:45.884
You can start with one.

00:09:45.884 --> 00:09:48.300
And the maximum you're allowed
in any one place is 10,000.

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:51.604
So I did a quick demo of running
10,000 copies of "Hello, world"

00:09:51.604 --> 00:09:52.270
because why not?

00:09:52.270 --> 00:09:53.000
MARK MANDEL: Why not.

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:53.874
JOHN WILKES: Exactly.

00:09:53.874 --> 00:09:55.400
And it took about
2 and 1/2 minutes

00:09:55.400 --> 00:09:58.227
to get from 0 to
10,000 copies, roughly.

00:09:58.227 --> 00:10:00.810
MARK MANDEL: You kind of slipped
something in there, where you

00:10:00.810 --> 00:10:03.672
said, it would likely to be.

00:10:03.672 --> 00:10:04.880
JOHN WILKES: Oh, you noticed.

00:10:04.880 --> 00:10:06.970
MARK MANDEL: I did.

00:10:06.970 --> 00:10:10.084
So it's not just saying, oh, I
want 10,000, so I get 10,000.

00:10:10.084 --> 00:10:11.500
It's something
slightly different.

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:15.407
So we have this notion of
things failing all the time.

00:10:15.407 --> 00:10:17.990
I'm on record as saying that the
reason Google looks like it's

00:10:17.990 --> 00:10:20.090
up from the outside is that we
assume the world is collapsing

00:10:20.090 --> 00:10:22.090
around our ears on the
inside and write software

00:10:22.090 --> 00:10:24.110
to cope for that happening.

00:10:24.110 --> 00:10:26.150
So one of the techniques
that we use for that

00:10:26.150 --> 00:10:28.420
is what we call those little
declarative statements

00:10:28.420 --> 00:10:30.034
about truth and
then reconciliation.

00:10:30.034 --> 00:10:31.700
So here, we have a
statement which says,

00:10:31.700 --> 00:10:33.740
I would like 10,000
copies of "Hello, world"

00:10:33.740 --> 00:10:34.500
running somewhere.

00:10:34.500 --> 00:10:36.590
And you send this off
to the Borg master

00:10:36.590 --> 00:10:38.830
and the Borg master
compares that with what

00:10:38.830 --> 00:10:41.710
is actually the state of the
world and goes, that's funny.

00:10:41.710 --> 00:10:43.790
Nobody's placed 10,000
copies of "Hello, world."

00:10:43.790 --> 00:10:45.120
So let me fix that.

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:47.370
So a component inside
the Borg master scheduler

00:10:47.370 --> 00:10:51.935
goes and starts looking for
places for those 10,000 copies.

00:10:51.935 --> 00:10:54.620
And remembers this fact, so the
other thing I need to point out

00:10:54.620 --> 00:10:56.420
is that what you
want to write down

00:10:56.420 --> 00:10:58.390
do your intentions,
your declarations,

00:10:58.390 --> 00:11:00.508
in a way that is
super resilient.

00:11:00.508 --> 00:11:02.550
So Borg master
uses a [INAUDIBLE],

00:11:02.550 --> 00:11:04.832
which a distributed
fault tolerant

00:11:04.832 --> 00:11:05.790
library implementation.

00:11:05.790 --> 00:11:08.810
So every copy of the Borg
master has a copy of this state,

00:11:08.810 --> 00:11:10.810
and there are five
copies in any one cell.

00:11:10.810 --> 00:11:11.850
So if any one of
them comes down,

00:11:11.850 --> 00:11:14.349
the others can pick up and carry
on, or at least another one

00:11:14.349 --> 00:11:16.030
can pick up and carry on.

00:11:16.030 --> 00:11:17.100
So you first of all write
down your intention--

00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:18.170
I want 10,000 copies.

00:11:18.170 --> 00:11:19.880
And then the scheduler
goes and picks off

00:11:19.880 --> 00:11:23.960
a few of these at a time and
finds places for them to run,

00:11:23.960 --> 00:11:26.819
spare machines in the
cell-- or, more likely,

00:11:26.819 --> 00:11:29.110
a fraction of the spare
machines somewhere in the cell.

00:11:29.110 --> 00:11:30.710
And it writes down his decision.

00:11:30.710 --> 00:11:32.270
Machine number 17 has a copy.

00:11:32.270 --> 00:11:34.172
Machine number 423 has a copy.

00:11:34.172 --> 00:11:36.630
And that also gets written out
in a persistent, declarative

00:11:36.630 --> 00:11:37.130
fashion.

00:11:37.130 --> 00:11:38.660
So if the scheduler
falls over, you

00:11:38.660 --> 00:11:41.630
can pick up and carry
on where you left off.

00:11:41.630 --> 00:11:43.200
And then, finally,
what happens is

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:45.241
that the Borg master itself
has a component which

00:11:45.241 --> 00:11:47.770
compares the state of
the world on the machines

00:11:47.770 --> 00:11:49.907
in that cell to what it
thinks should be true.

00:11:49.907 --> 00:11:51.490
That's funny, that
machine is supposed

00:11:51.490 --> 00:11:52.910
to have a copy of
this "Hello, world,"

00:11:52.910 --> 00:11:53.910
but it doesn't have one.

00:11:53.910 --> 00:11:55.130
Let me fix that.

00:11:55.130 --> 00:11:57.760
And it goes ahead and tells an
agent sitting on that machine

00:11:57.760 --> 00:11:59.620
to go grab itself a
copy of "Hello, world"

00:11:59.620 --> 00:12:01.010
and start running it.

00:12:01.010 --> 00:12:03.990
So that loop of comparing the
outside world against what

00:12:03.990 --> 00:12:07.247
you want to be true turns out
to be remarkably resilient

00:12:07.247 --> 00:12:08.080
in case of failures.

00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:10.170
You can sort of fall over
anywhere in the process

00:12:10.170 --> 00:12:12.545
and then pick up from where
you left off and sort of just

00:12:12.545 --> 00:12:13.920
get back to where
you want to be.

00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:15.336
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Very interesting.

00:12:15.336 --> 00:12:17.900
That means that, at the
beginning, we're going to say,

00:12:17.900 --> 00:12:20.050
oh, we're missing 10,000 copies.

00:12:20.050 --> 00:12:23.960
But then later on, we're going
to be, we have, like, 9,000.

00:12:23.960 --> 00:12:26.180
We're missing only 1,000.

00:12:26.180 --> 00:12:31.550
And at what point do we
consider [INAUDIBLE] stable?

00:12:31.550 --> 00:12:35.431
And how long-- like you said, 2
minutes and a half for 10,000?

00:12:35.431 --> 00:12:37.680
JOHN WILKES: So it took from
zero to 2 and 1/2 minutes

00:12:37.680 --> 00:12:39.800
to get up to almost 10,000.

00:12:39.800 --> 00:12:42.700
And it turns out that the
Borg master is always trying

00:12:42.700 --> 00:12:44.444
to make 10,000 copies running.

00:12:44.444 --> 00:12:46.610
But typically it happens
that there's a few of them.

00:12:46.610 --> 00:12:49.520
And when I was looking, I
saw about seven to 10 of them

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:53.560
were not running,
even in steady state.

00:12:53.560 --> 00:12:54.930
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Interesting.

00:12:54.930 --> 00:12:56.888
MARK MANDEL: Is that just
a problem because you

00:12:56.888 --> 00:12:57.830
have 10,000 of them?

00:12:57.830 --> 00:13:00.780
Or is there something actively
saying, no, maybe, "Hello,

00:13:00.780 --> 00:13:04.130
world" isn't the most
useful thing in the world

00:13:04.130 --> 00:13:05.720
to be running 10,000 copies of?

00:13:05.720 --> 00:13:07.315
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That is
important to say hi, though.

00:13:07.315 --> 00:13:08.606
JOHN WILKES: My "Hello, world?"

00:13:08.606 --> 00:13:09.412
Are you crazy?

00:13:09.412 --> 00:13:10.140
MARK MANDEL: I understand.

00:13:10.140 --> 00:13:11.776
JOHN WILKES: Of
course it's important.

00:13:11.776 --> 00:13:14.591
I'm giving demo.

00:13:14.591 --> 00:13:16.340
No, it turns out,
actually, "Hello, world"

00:13:16.340 --> 00:13:18.173
was the least important
thing in the system.

00:13:18.173 --> 00:13:21.170
And that matters for
what I'm about to say.

00:13:21.170 --> 00:13:23.900
Things go wrong all the time.

00:13:23.900 --> 00:13:26.040
Occasionally, machines break.

00:13:26.040 --> 00:13:28.190
When you've got 10,000
of them in a cell,

00:13:28.190 --> 00:13:30.650
they will actually break
at a break at a fair clip,

00:13:30.650 --> 00:13:31.400
in absolute terms.

00:13:31.400 --> 00:13:33.858
But in relative terms, these
things are amazingly reliable.

00:13:33.858 --> 00:13:35.970
They'll stay up for
quite a long time.

00:13:35.970 --> 00:13:37.860
However, we will take
the operating system

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:39.460
down roughly once
a month in order

00:13:39.460 --> 00:13:41.684
to do a software upgrade--
maybe because we've

00:13:41.684 --> 00:13:43.600
got a better version of
kernel, or maybe we're

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:45.610
rolling out some security
update-- all sorts

00:13:45.610 --> 00:13:47.574
of good reasons for doing this.

00:13:47.574 --> 00:13:49.240
When you take the
operating system down,

00:13:49.240 --> 00:13:50.745
you basically have to
reboot the machine.

00:13:50.745 --> 00:13:52.730
And to reboot the machine,
all the stuff that

00:13:52.730 --> 00:13:55.150
was running on it is
going to have a bad time.

00:13:55.150 --> 00:13:57.330
So that basically is
the primary driver

00:13:57.330 --> 00:14:00.170
if you're a production task--
imagine, something like Gmail,

00:14:00.170 --> 00:14:04.570
or Google Docs, or Google
cloud platform products.

00:14:04.570 --> 00:14:07.525
Those things we'll see an
outage roughly once a month

00:14:07.525 --> 00:14:10.672
if they are running
all the time.

00:14:10.672 --> 00:14:12.130
That's OK for them,
because they're

00:14:12.130 --> 00:14:13.590
used to having other
copies somewhere else.

00:14:13.590 --> 00:14:14.210
So it's not a problem.

00:14:14.210 --> 00:14:15.310
You just write software
to cope with the fact

00:14:15.310 --> 00:14:16.435
that it's going to go away.

00:14:16.435 --> 00:14:19.170
But it means that there is
a sort of slow, gentle churn

00:14:19.170 --> 00:14:20.766
of things being removed.

00:14:20.766 --> 00:14:22.890
Now that's for what we call
production stuff, which

00:14:22.890 --> 00:14:24.980
is the things we
really care about.

00:14:24.980 --> 00:14:27.789
We have other things, like batch
jobs, which are less important,

00:14:27.789 --> 00:14:29.830
or at least maybe they're
more tolerant of things

00:14:29.830 --> 00:14:31.496
going wrong-- is the
way to think of it.

00:14:31.496 --> 00:14:34.260
And because they are more
tolerant-- if a MapReduce loses

00:14:34.260 --> 00:14:35.180
the work, that's fine.

00:14:35.180 --> 00:14:37.054
There's plenty more
where that one came from.

00:14:37.054 --> 00:14:39.880
And maybe we'll bring this
one back and get another copy.

00:14:39.880 --> 00:14:41.885
And maybe it'll be
delayed a second or so.

00:14:41.885 --> 00:14:45.340
Eh, no big deal, if it's
running for six hours.

00:14:45.340 --> 00:14:46.770
It gives them more flexibility.

00:14:46.770 --> 00:14:50.240
So we'll actually allow them
to get pushed out of the way

00:14:50.240 --> 00:14:52.489
if we need space for
production tasks to run.

00:14:52.489 --> 00:14:54.780
So if you're a production
task, you have high priority.

00:14:54.780 --> 00:14:56.988
If you have high priority,
and there's a low priority

00:14:56.988 --> 00:14:58.470
thing in your way, you win.

00:14:58.470 --> 00:15:00.440
The low priority
thing gets bumped off.

00:15:00.440 --> 00:15:01.346
A task dies.

00:15:01.346 --> 00:15:02.970
And it will get
started somewhere else,

00:15:02.970 --> 00:15:03.636
if there's room.

00:15:03.636 --> 00:15:04.330
That's fine.

00:15:04.330 --> 00:15:06.080
And you know, there's
this priority order.

00:15:06.080 --> 00:15:09.030
It goes from sort of 200 issues
where we run production stuff,

00:15:09.030 --> 00:15:11.820
down to zero, which is the
lowest of the low, which

00:15:11.820 --> 00:15:13.840
is where "Hello,
world" was running.

00:15:13.840 --> 00:15:16.910
So if any other task in the
system needed a resource that

00:15:16.910 --> 00:15:19.610
my "Hello, world" was sitting
on, "Hello, " got moved.

00:15:19.610 --> 00:15:20.400
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Goodbye "Hello, world."

00:15:20.400 --> 00:15:21.870
JOHN WILKES: Goodbye
"Hello, world."

00:15:21.870 --> 00:15:22.320
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very cool.

00:15:22.320 --> 00:15:25.210
So most of the things that
you've been talking about I

00:15:25.210 --> 00:15:29.360
have the impression that were
limited to a single cell.

00:15:29.360 --> 00:15:31.750
Is there one Borg per cell?

00:15:31.750 --> 00:15:34.190
Is the Borg in all of the cells?

00:15:34.190 --> 00:15:35.360
How does that work?

00:15:35.360 --> 00:15:37.526
JOHN WILKES: Oh, this might
be a great point to say,

00:15:37.526 --> 00:15:39.840
this Borg is an
internal code name.

00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:41.840
It's not an external trade name.

00:15:41.840 --> 00:15:43.340
The lawyers can be
happy about this.

00:15:43.340 --> 00:15:46.460
So it's just an
internal code name.

00:15:46.460 --> 00:15:50.450
We try to avoid the Borg and the
associations that go with that.

00:15:50.450 --> 00:15:56.090
Sorry, bad choice of name,
before my time, not my fault.

00:15:56.090 --> 00:15:57.382
I talked about we have a fleet.

00:15:57.382 --> 00:15:59.173
I'm not going to say
how big, but it's big.

00:15:59.173 --> 00:16:00.400
We divide it up into chunks.

00:16:00.400 --> 00:16:02.450
Those chunks are
designed to be big enough

00:16:02.450 --> 00:16:04.960
to run some of the largest
applications we need to run

00:16:04.960 --> 00:16:08.040
but small enough that, if you
lose an entire chunk because, I

00:16:08.040 --> 00:16:10.580
don't know, software bug, or
one of our site reliability

00:16:10.580 --> 00:16:13.640
engineers mistypes the name
of one of these things,

00:16:13.640 --> 00:16:15.260
then it's actually not too bad.

00:16:15.260 --> 00:16:17.272
Which is why 10,000 is
an interesting number.

00:16:17.272 --> 00:16:19.730
It's big enough to be able to
get a lot of work done in one

00:16:19.730 --> 00:16:22.188
place, and it's small enough
that you can have two or three

00:16:22.188 --> 00:16:25.300
copies of your application
running in different places.

00:16:25.300 --> 00:16:29.650
So one of the reasons why
these Borg clumps, these cells,

00:16:29.650 --> 00:16:32.430
are the size they are is
to allow sort of failures

00:16:32.430 --> 00:16:35.190
and to bound the
scope of a mistake.

00:16:35.190 --> 00:16:38.020
The other is so you can do
things like software updates.

00:16:38.020 --> 00:16:39.924
Take them down, bring
them back up again.

00:16:39.924 --> 00:16:42.340
So the thing is designed to
cope with pretty much anything

00:16:42.340 --> 00:16:43.890
disappearing at any moment.

00:16:43.890 --> 00:16:46.500
For example, if the Borg
master has a software bug

00:16:46.500 --> 00:16:48.230
and disappears, all
the stuff that's

00:16:48.230 --> 00:16:50.110
working in the cell
right now stays working.

00:16:50.110 --> 00:16:51.185
It doesn't stop.

00:16:51.185 --> 00:16:53.170
And the agents on
each of the machines

00:16:53.170 --> 00:16:55.310
that will trying to
contact to keep trying.

00:16:55.310 --> 00:16:56.270
And eventually,
it will come back,

00:16:56.270 --> 00:16:58.050
and they'll just pick
up where they left off.

00:16:58.050 --> 00:16:59.591
So in the meantime,
they'll just keep

00:16:59.591 --> 00:17:02.790
working on what it was
they were last asked to do.

00:17:02.790 --> 00:17:04.870
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, we
kind of keep going around

00:17:04.870 --> 00:17:05.869
it and talking about it.

00:17:05.869 --> 00:17:08.368
I kind of want to highlight it
as a really interesting point

00:17:08.368 --> 00:17:10.640
that everything is
built for failure,

00:17:10.640 --> 00:17:12.660
like it an assumption
of failure.

00:17:12.660 --> 00:17:15.020
Everything will
break at some point.

00:17:15.020 --> 00:17:17.380
And I think that's a really
interesting like software

00:17:17.380 --> 00:17:20.740
development practice to start
from, like, everything is bad.

00:17:20.740 --> 00:17:22.550
Rather than, in
an ideal scenario,

00:17:22.550 --> 00:17:25.069
the server will be up forever.

00:17:25.069 --> 00:17:26.930
I think that's really,
really interesting.

00:17:26.930 --> 00:17:28.380
JOHN WILKES: So a lot of
the design discussions

00:17:28.380 --> 00:17:29.850
that I get involved
in are around,

00:17:29.850 --> 00:17:32.050
what happens when that
fails while you're

00:17:32.050 --> 00:17:33.620
doing this other thing?

00:17:33.620 --> 00:17:35.900
Have you made a slightly
flaky assumption

00:17:35.900 --> 00:17:40.000
that it's going to be OK because
it's going to keep working?

00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:42.336
I was in a room one time
listening to a talk of somebody

00:17:42.336 --> 00:17:43.960
describing one of
our internal systems,

00:17:43.960 --> 00:17:46.700
and he was sort of skating
over one of these areas.

00:17:46.700 --> 00:17:49.290
And there were 50 or
60 people in the room.

00:17:49.290 --> 00:17:50.830
And somebody asked
him a question

00:17:50.830 --> 00:17:51.540
about a particular thing.

00:17:51.540 --> 00:17:52.490
It turned out that
they'd allowed

00:17:52.490 --> 00:17:53.420
a [? raised ?]
condition to happen,

00:17:53.420 --> 00:17:55.337
and there was a point
where it could go wrong.

00:17:55.337 --> 00:17:57.461
And I think about a third
of the people in the room

00:17:57.461 --> 00:17:59.819
put their hand up at that
point simultaneously and said,

00:17:59.819 --> 00:18:01.090
just a minute.

00:18:01.090 --> 00:18:02.122
MARK MANDEL: No, no.

00:18:02.122 --> 00:18:04.337
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Wait a minute.

00:18:04.337 --> 00:18:05.670
MARK MANDEL: That's really cool.

00:18:05.670 --> 00:18:06.700
So that's really interesting.

00:18:06.700 --> 00:18:08.283
So you've got this
declarative syntax.

00:18:08.283 --> 00:18:10.987
You tell your system
what it is you want.

00:18:10.987 --> 00:18:13.320
That means you have to have
a lot of trust in the system

00:18:13.320 --> 00:18:14.530
that you're running on.

00:18:14.530 --> 00:18:16.490
How does that get verified?

00:18:16.490 --> 00:18:18.840
Or do you just trust it,
and you're like, it's fine?

00:18:18.840 --> 00:18:20.506
But I mean, you're
building for failure.

00:18:20.506 --> 00:18:21.470
So how does that work?

00:18:21.470 --> 00:18:23.720
JOHN WILKES: There's a slogan
which really works here.

00:18:23.720 --> 00:18:26.130
If you're not measuring
it, it is out of control.

00:18:26.130 --> 00:18:28.070
So trust, but verify.

00:18:28.070 --> 00:18:30.767
We do put a lot of effort
into designing systems

00:18:30.767 --> 00:18:32.850
to be failure tolerant,
including the systems that

00:18:32.850 --> 00:18:34.030
run the systems
that are designed

00:18:34.030 --> 00:18:35.446
to be failure
tolerant, and so on,

00:18:35.446 --> 00:18:36.880
all the way up and
down the stack.

00:18:36.880 --> 00:18:38.240
But you want to make
sure that it is.

00:18:38.240 --> 00:18:39.573
You actually need to measure it.

00:18:39.573 --> 00:18:42.490
So we put a huge
amount of resources

00:18:42.490 --> 00:18:44.790
into monitoring the
systems that we have,

00:18:44.790 --> 00:18:47.940
including the systems that are
monitoring the systems we have.

00:18:47.940 --> 00:18:49.550
And they will report
things like, this

00:18:49.550 --> 00:18:50.841
is what I was expecting to see.

00:18:50.841 --> 00:18:52.759
This is what I'm
currently seeing.

00:18:52.759 --> 00:18:54.550
And if they differ in
the wrong way around,

00:18:54.550 --> 00:18:57.900
then you fire off alerts
and get people involved.

00:18:57.900 --> 00:19:00.410
People are always at
the top of the stack.

00:19:00.410 --> 00:19:02.642
People can do things that
no machine can ever do.

00:19:02.642 --> 00:19:04.350
But you want to have
the machine automate

00:19:04.350 --> 00:19:07.230
the process of doing the
regular straightforward stuff.

00:19:07.230 --> 00:19:08.980
For example, we're
talking only about sort

00:19:08.980 --> 00:19:12.900
of tasks being killed or pushed
off a machine, this is normal.

00:19:12.900 --> 00:19:14.860
You don't have to write
special code to handle

00:19:14.860 --> 00:19:16.130
this as an unusual exception.

00:19:16.130 --> 00:19:17.214
This happens all the time.

00:19:17.214 --> 00:19:19.671
So you write the software to
have that happen all the time,

00:19:19.671 --> 00:19:20.600
and then you move on.

00:19:20.600 --> 00:19:21.730
And then you check
that, in fact,

00:19:21.730 --> 00:19:23.146
if you asked for
100 copies of it,

00:19:23.146 --> 00:19:24.667
you got close to
100 copies running.

00:19:24.667 --> 00:19:26.250
But there is a
monitoring system which

00:19:26.250 --> 00:19:28.624
is keeping an eye on that,
and displaying it, and telling

00:19:28.624 --> 00:19:30.540
eventually a human,
or maybe [INAUDIBLE]

00:19:30.540 --> 00:19:32.690
another intermediate
system, that you

00:19:32.690 --> 00:19:35.030
have fewer than you expected.

00:19:35.030 --> 00:19:36.980
Now, to make that
work, the people

00:19:36.980 --> 00:19:38.660
who write the software
on which you rely

00:19:38.660 --> 00:19:41.850
typically have to tell you
how much you can rely on it.

00:19:41.850 --> 00:19:44.090
So we have internal
specifications

00:19:44.090 --> 00:19:46.240
of how often things break.

00:19:46.240 --> 00:19:49.560
For example, we have a sort
of rate limit, or rather

00:19:49.560 --> 00:19:52.410
an expectation,
of how frequently

00:19:52.410 --> 00:19:54.772
your tasks can be removed.

00:19:54.772 --> 00:19:56.980
So you write your software
assuming that that rate is

00:19:56.980 --> 00:19:58.330
probably going to be net.

00:19:58.330 --> 00:20:00.490
And then we have the site
reliability engineers

00:20:00.490 --> 00:20:01.570
who run these systems.

00:20:01.570 --> 00:20:03.784
And they're spectacularly
good at making

00:20:03.784 --> 00:20:05.700
sure the systems both
do what they're supposed

00:20:05.700 --> 00:20:07.560
to do and do it within spec.

00:20:07.560 --> 00:20:10.339
And they invoke
fixes and do whatever

00:20:10.339 --> 00:20:12.380
they need to do if it
turns out the system is not

00:20:12.380 --> 00:20:13.705
behaving as it should be.

00:20:13.705 --> 00:20:16.163
And the only way they know that
is if they're monitoring it

00:20:16.163 --> 00:20:17.097
and keep an eye on it.

00:20:17.097 --> 00:20:18.680
But that gives you
a handle on design.

00:20:18.680 --> 00:20:22.817
So you know that there's no
unbounded limit at which things

00:20:22.817 --> 00:20:23.650
are going to go bad.

00:20:23.650 --> 00:20:26.650
We will tell you, here is
a plausible upper bound

00:20:26.650 --> 00:20:29.280
on how bad things could be,
and write software around

00:20:29.280 --> 00:20:30.770
according to that.

00:20:30.770 --> 00:20:32.340
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So that is actually

00:20:32.340 --> 00:20:34.660
something very interesting.

00:20:34.660 --> 00:20:37.490
Is that what, at
bigger scale, becomes

00:20:37.490 --> 00:20:41.170
what we call an SLA, a
Service Level Agreement?

00:20:41.170 --> 00:20:43.570
JOHN WILKES: Well, it becomes
the SLO part of the SLA.

00:20:43.570 --> 00:20:44.070
FRANCESC CAMPOY: SLO, yeah.

00:20:44.070 --> 00:20:45.736
JOHN WILKES: Right,
so just terminology,

00:20:45.736 --> 00:20:47.910
for [INAUDIBLE],
Service Level Objective.

00:20:47.910 --> 00:20:48.410
FRANCESC CAMPOY: There you go.

00:20:48.410 --> 00:20:49.993
JOHN WILKES: The way
I think of it is,

00:20:49.993 --> 00:20:52.140
something I want to be true.

00:20:52.140 --> 00:20:55.270
I'd love there to be no more
than about this many failures

00:20:55.270 --> 00:20:55.990
per week.

00:20:55.990 --> 00:20:57.865
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And
something that I thought

00:20:57.865 --> 00:21:00.130
was pretty amazing when I
was reading the SRE book,

00:21:00.130 --> 00:21:04.140
it was about SLO's and how,
if we are having our SLO

00:21:04.140 --> 00:21:06.710
is higher than
expected, sometimes we

00:21:06.710 --> 00:21:10.852
will just make our
services fail just for fun.

00:21:10.852 --> 00:21:12.815
JOHN WILKES: For two reasons.

00:21:12.815 --> 00:21:14.398
Well, some people
might do it for fun.

00:21:17.122 --> 00:21:19.580
MARK MANDEL: Some people just
want to watch the world burn.

00:21:19.580 --> 00:21:23.480
JOHN WILKES: Exactly.
[JOKER LAUGH]

00:21:23.480 --> 00:21:25.420
There's a couple of reasons.

00:21:25.420 --> 00:21:28.470
Sometimes you find people
mistakenly assuming something

00:21:28.470 --> 00:21:29.910
is going to be up.

00:21:29.910 --> 00:21:32.340
Because it always has been
whenever they've looked.

00:21:32.340 --> 00:21:34.687
Well, if it turns
out it might not be,

00:21:34.687 --> 00:21:37.145
and they've made an assumption
that it is, that's a failure

00:21:37.145 --> 00:21:39.037
that they've just
written for themselves.

00:21:39.037 --> 00:21:40.870
So, for example, we
have a system internally

00:21:40.870 --> 00:21:42.680
we call Chubby, which
we use for storing

00:21:42.680 --> 00:21:44.670
relatively small quantities
of information that

00:21:44.670 --> 00:21:46.357
is incredibly available.

00:21:46.357 --> 00:21:48.315
And we have local versions
of it in every cell,

00:21:48.315 --> 00:21:49.590
or pretty much every cell.

00:21:49.590 --> 00:21:51.089
And we also have a
global one, which

00:21:51.089 --> 00:21:53.830
is more convenient to
access, but we do not

00:21:53.830 --> 00:21:54.990
promise it is always there.

00:21:54.990 --> 00:21:57.160
So just to make sure that,
if you choose to use it,

00:21:57.160 --> 00:22:00.110
you cannot rely on it, the
people who run that service

00:22:00.110 --> 00:22:02.545
will deliberately take
it down once a quarter.

00:22:02.545 --> 00:22:03.920
And then we find
all those people

00:22:03.920 --> 00:22:06.419
who fall over because they were
assuming that old Chubby was

00:22:06.419 --> 00:22:08.990
there, and we go fix them
because that's not acceptable.

00:22:08.990 --> 00:22:11.420
So that's one reason for why
you might choose to inject

00:22:11.420 --> 00:22:13.760
failures-- to find faults.

00:22:13.760 --> 00:22:16.670
And the second reason
is that, maybe if you're

00:22:16.670 --> 00:22:18.200
making your system
too good, you're

00:22:18.200 --> 00:22:20.290
spending too much money on it.

00:22:20.290 --> 00:22:21.290
You can maybe economize.

00:22:21.290 --> 00:22:22.790
You could maybe
use fewer resources

00:22:22.790 --> 00:22:25.220
or maybe have a few
less copies, or be

00:22:25.220 --> 00:22:27.280
willing to take the
resources you are putting

00:22:27.280 --> 00:22:29.220
into that system to
make it behave well

00:22:29.220 --> 00:22:31.678
and move it somewhere else
where it would have more benefit

00:22:31.678 --> 00:22:33.344
to a business or a customer.

00:22:33.344 --> 00:22:35.510
MARK MANDEL: I guess it's
kind of that whole thing--

00:22:35.510 --> 00:22:37.170
it's better to do
it like in training,

00:22:37.170 --> 00:22:39.250
or when people are watching,
for a bad thing to happen,

00:22:39.250 --> 00:22:41.000
than to have to go,
oh, it's an emergency.

00:22:41.000 --> 00:22:43.020
Let's do it right now.

00:22:43.020 --> 00:22:44.120
JOHN WILKES: Absolutely.

00:22:44.120 --> 00:22:45.953
But you also have to
prepare for emergencies

00:22:45.953 --> 00:22:47.550
and be ready to do
it right now, which

00:22:47.550 --> 00:22:49.716
is why having people
incredibly good at this running

00:22:49.716 --> 00:22:52.100
your systems is the
right way to go.

00:22:52.100 --> 00:22:55.750
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So regarding
that issue of making sure

00:22:55.750 --> 00:22:59.220
that things don't rely on
things that could go down,

00:22:59.220 --> 00:23:03.490
is it also true that sometimes
we will take down actual data

00:23:03.490 --> 00:23:06.910
centers, or things like that--
not just for fun, as we said,

00:23:06.910 --> 00:23:10.890
but to make sure that not
like not crazy assumptions

00:23:10.890 --> 00:23:11.651
are taken.

00:23:11.651 --> 00:23:13.400
JOHN WILKES: So we do
this annual exercise

00:23:13.400 --> 00:23:16.110
we call Dirt, where
we, first of all,

00:23:16.110 --> 00:23:18.510
begin by assuming that the
company headquarters has slid

00:23:18.510 --> 00:23:21.426
into the sea in California
and is disconnected

00:23:21.426 --> 00:23:22.550
from the rest of the world.

00:23:22.550 --> 00:23:25.020
So can we keep running
Google without Mountain View?

00:23:25.020 --> 00:23:27.179
And the answer,
pretty much, is yes.

00:23:27.179 --> 00:23:28.970
And then we add some
interesting mix to it.

00:23:28.970 --> 00:23:32.460
So my favorite from one year
was that the operations team

00:23:32.460 --> 00:23:34.730
in Dublin apparently
went out drinking one day

00:23:34.730 --> 00:23:37.950
and wasn't going to be
back for several days.

00:23:37.950 --> 00:23:40.080
Another version of
this is, we simulate--

00:23:40.080 --> 00:23:41.215
we didn't actually do this.

00:23:41.215 --> 00:23:42.131
That would be painful.

00:23:42.131 --> 00:23:43.030
It's a simulation.

00:23:43.030 --> 00:23:45.740
Let's pretend we have
lost all power to one

00:23:45.740 --> 00:23:48.454
of our really big data centers.

00:23:48.454 --> 00:23:50.120
And you discover
what's going to happen.

00:23:50.120 --> 00:23:51.820
Well, what happened
is, those people go,

00:23:51.820 --> 00:23:53.778
good grief, we've got a
bunch of stuff running.

00:23:53.778 --> 00:23:55.090
We have diesel generators.

00:23:55.090 --> 00:23:57.830
They switch on.

00:23:57.830 --> 00:23:59.440
They start burning
through diesel

00:23:59.440 --> 00:24:00.690
to keep these things running.

00:24:00.690 --> 00:24:03.690
They're producing an
awful lot of power.

00:24:03.690 --> 00:24:05.750
And you call up the
local diesel suppliers

00:24:05.750 --> 00:24:07.700
and say, give me
trucks full of diesel.

00:24:07.700 --> 00:24:09.450
Get them lined up right
now, because we're

00:24:09.450 --> 00:24:11.290
going to need to move
them as fast as we

00:24:11.290 --> 00:24:12.541
can to keep the things afloat.

00:24:12.541 --> 00:24:13.956
And then somebody
says, great, how

00:24:13.956 --> 00:24:15.368
are you going to pay for this?

00:24:15.368 --> 00:24:21.760
And you say, oh, the people
in Mountain View-- oops.

00:24:21.760 --> 00:24:23.500
So that's why you
do these exercises,

00:24:23.500 --> 00:24:26.010
is you have a chance to find
out those things in a world

00:24:26.010 --> 00:24:28.210
where it isn't
actually a disaster.

00:24:28.210 --> 00:24:31.320
You can cope with it ahead of
time and go, let's fix that.

00:24:31.320 --> 00:24:33.990
Another example where
something happened was we

00:24:33.990 --> 00:24:36.777
discovered we needed to get
everybody on a phone bridge

00:24:36.777 --> 00:24:39.110
to be able to talk about it
because we have systems that

00:24:39.110 --> 00:24:40.310
are spread across the
globe, and we have

00:24:40.310 --> 00:24:41.601
experts in different countries.

00:24:41.601 --> 00:24:43.956
And they are panicking
trying to get something done.

00:24:43.956 --> 00:24:45.580
And they couldn't
set up a phone bridge

00:24:45.580 --> 00:24:48.121
because that relied on a piece
of software that was currently

00:24:48.121 --> 00:24:48.704
down.

00:24:48.704 --> 00:24:49.620
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:24:49.620 --> 00:24:51.140
JOHN WILKES: The fix for that
is quite straightforward.

00:24:51.140 --> 00:24:53.360
You always have a phone
bridge ready to go.

00:24:53.360 --> 00:24:55.151
And everybody has its
number that needs it.

00:24:55.151 --> 00:24:55.784
And off you go.

00:24:55.784 --> 00:24:57.450
So those are the kind
of things that you

00:24:57.450 --> 00:24:59.667
don't think of until you
go through the process.

00:24:59.667 --> 00:25:01.750
And then you go, d'oh, why
didn't I think of that?

00:25:05.440 --> 00:25:07.180
As you build up
experience in this,

00:25:07.180 --> 00:25:09.540
and I think the SRE book is
a wonderful example of that,

00:25:09.540 --> 00:25:12.260
it's basically a distillation
of several thousand people

00:25:12.260 --> 00:25:15.010
times a dozen plus
years of experiences

00:25:15.010 --> 00:25:17.590
of making or discovering
opportunities

00:25:17.590 --> 00:25:20.210
for not screwing up and writing
them down for other people

00:25:20.210 --> 00:25:21.670
so we could spread the wealth.

00:25:21.670 --> 00:25:23.670
MARK MANDEL: Standing on
the shoulder of giants.

00:25:23.670 --> 00:25:25.045
JOHN WILKES: There we go, yes.

00:25:25.045 --> 00:25:27.300
MARK MANDEL: So let's
change tack a little bit.

00:25:27.300 --> 00:25:29.591
I don't want to go over time,
as much as I want to just

00:25:29.591 --> 00:25:31.160
talk to you forever and ever.

00:25:31.160 --> 00:25:33.076
We want to talk a little
about the open source

00:25:33.076 --> 00:25:34.420
project Kubernetes.

00:25:34.420 --> 00:25:37.280
There's definitely direct
influences from Borg,

00:25:37.280 --> 00:25:39.830
and what we do at Google
as a whole, to Kubernetes--

00:25:39.830 --> 00:25:41.870
I think, specifically
around lessons

00:25:41.870 --> 00:25:46.506
around failure, optimization,
trusting your system.

00:25:46.506 --> 00:25:48.880
Do you know some history about
how Kubernetes came about?

00:25:48.880 --> 00:25:50.546
And I think you
probably heard about it.

00:25:50.546 --> 00:25:51.755
What's that connection there?

00:25:51.755 --> 00:25:53.212
JOHN WILKES: So
indeed, yes, it was

00:25:53.212 --> 00:25:55.540
some people who were associated
with Borg ecosystem who

00:25:55.540 --> 00:25:56.540
were involved in its creation.

00:25:56.540 --> 00:25:58.165
So it came out as a
combination of sort

00:25:58.165 --> 00:26:00.260
of Borg and the cloud
ecosystem that we

00:26:00.260 --> 00:26:01.690
were building internally.

00:26:01.690 --> 00:26:07.047
And somebody noticed that this
wonderful use of containers, we

00:26:07.047 --> 00:26:08.630
use them internally,
we have used them

00:26:08.630 --> 00:26:10.230
for a very long
time-- Borg is built

00:26:10.230 --> 00:26:12.012
around allocating containers.

00:26:12.012 --> 00:26:14.220
They are just are a way of
encapsulating your program

00:26:14.220 --> 00:26:15.944
so that it can run
pretty much anywhere.

00:26:15.944 --> 00:26:18.360
I talked before about running
on the desktop and then also

00:26:18.360 --> 00:26:20.052
being able to move
into Borg universe.

00:26:20.052 --> 00:26:22.510
The same thing is now possible
in the external world thanks

00:26:22.510 --> 00:26:24.004
to Docker, which
is just a really

00:26:24.004 --> 00:26:26.420
nice, tasteful combination of
a bunch of technologies that

00:26:26.420 --> 00:26:28.320
were floating around that
somebody went and said,

00:26:28.320 --> 00:26:30.153
good grief, if we put
these things together,

00:26:30.153 --> 00:26:31.910
we've got essentially
the same idea.

00:26:31.910 --> 00:26:33.290
So Docker came out
in the outside world.

00:26:33.290 --> 00:26:35.030
And about the same time,
sort of our cloud business

00:26:35.030 --> 00:26:36.920
was getting going in
an interesting way.

00:26:36.920 --> 00:26:38.820
And we went, ah,
wonderful opportunity

00:26:38.820 --> 00:26:41.690
for us to share our
expertise to make

00:26:41.690 --> 00:26:43.810
this available to
the larger ecosystem.

00:26:43.810 --> 00:26:46.560
So if you've go one instance
of one application running

00:26:46.560 --> 00:26:48.977
in one place, then a Docker
container is just fine.

00:26:48.977 --> 00:26:50.310
You can launch new copies of it.

00:26:50.310 --> 00:26:51.101
You can send it up.

00:26:51.101 --> 00:26:52.580
You can test it.

00:26:52.580 --> 00:26:54.860
Once you get to about 10,
or 100 of these things,

00:26:54.860 --> 00:26:56.860
it gets tedious to
do this by hand.

00:26:56.860 --> 00:26:58.660
Hold on, we've done that before.

00:26:58.660 --> 00:27:00.190
There's a pattern
we've seen here.

00:27:00.190 --> 00:27:01.940
Let's see if we can
replicate that pattern

00:27:01.940 --> 00:27:04.070
in the outside world
using off the shelf

00:27:04.070 --> 00:27:06.160
software in the open
source community

00:27:06.160 --> 00:27:07.910
and make it available
to people so that we

00:27:07.910 --> 00:27:10.076
can build an ecosystem and
have everybody contribute

00:27:10.076 --> 00:27:12.160
to this so it's not
just a Google project,

00:27:12.160 --> 00:27:14.170
it's actually an open
source community that's

00:27:14.170 --> 00:27:15.260
been created around the stuff.

00:27:15.260 --> 00:27:17.218
We have lots of contributors,
thousands of them

00:27:17.218 --> 00:27:19.970
at the last count, trying to
help Kubernetes become better.

00:27:19.970 --> 00:27:21.576
Kubernetes is the outcome.

00:27:21.576 --> 00:27:22.950
One way of looking
at it is, it's

00:27:22.950 --> 00:27:28.000
a sort of Borg Lite version
2 done to try and take

00:27:28.000 --> 00:27:29.920
the most important
parts of the lessons

00:27:29.920 --> 00:27:32.420
we've learned-- including things
like the failure tolerance.

00:27:32.420 --> 00:27:37.274
So Borg uses [INAUDIBLE] for
its internal persistent store.

00:27:37.274 --> 00:27:40.577
Kubernetes uses [INAUDIBLE]
to get the same effect.

00:27:40.577 --> 00:27:42.910
There's a central place where
you have the master in it,

00:27:42.910 --> 00:27:45.002
you say, I wish this to
be true, and the same kind

00:27:45.002 --> 00:27:47.210
of protocols that we talked
about before are applied.

00:27:47.210 --> 00:27:48.610
There's an agent
[INAUDIBLE] machine

00:27:48.610 --> 00:27:50.000
called the Kublet, much
same way that there's

00:27:50.000 --> 00:27:51.999
a Borg [INAUDIBLE] machine
in the Borg universe.

00:27:51.999 --> 00:27:54.027
So all those ideas
are very similar.

00:27:54.027 --> 00:27:55.610
And then we added a
few new ones based

00:27:55.610 --> 00:27:56.515
on our experience with Borg.

00:27:56.515 --> 00:27:58.790
And we've been running this
thing for a decade or so now.

00:27:58.790 --> 00:28:00.460
And we've learned there are
a few things it does that we

00:28:00.460 --> 00:28:01.210
wished it didn't.

00:28:01.210 --> 00:28:04.730
So let's not impose that
on the outside world.

00:28:04.730 --> 00:28:06.160
Only new mistakes.

00:28:06.160 --> 00:28:07.390
So that's really how it came.

00:28:07.390 --> 00:28:09.848
And it turned out a lot of the
people who are now currently

00:28:09.848 --> 00:28:14.030
on the Google portion
of the Kubernetes effort

00:28:14.030 --> 00:28:16.730
are ex-Borg people.

00:28:16.730 --> 00:28:19.880
They either moved 20
feet down the corridor

00:28:19.880 --> 00:28:22.390
or stayed where they were
and then switched projects

00:28:22.390 --> 00:28:24.848
and understood they're applying
the expertise they built up

00:28:24.848 --> 00:28:26.690
over the years in
the Borg universe

00:28:26.690 --> 00:28:28.040
to the Kubernetes space.

00:28:28.040 --> 00:28:30.260
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
that is interesting.

00:28:30.260 --> 00:28:32.260
You said that we
could see Kubernetes

00:28:32.260 --> 00:28:37.290
as kind of an evolution of
Borg with all the things we

00:28:37.290 --> 00:28:38.700
have learned over the years.

00:28:38.700 --> 00:28:43.030
What is the biggest difference
you think is worth mentioning?

00:28:43.030 --> 00:28:44.440
JOHN WILKES: It's open source.

00:28:44.440 --> 00:28:46.070
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:28:46.070 --> 00:28:47.775
That is a very good point.

00:28:47.775 --> 00:28:49.400
JOHN WILKES: People
every now and again

00:28:49.400 --> 00:28:51.140
ask me, so, why didn't
you open source Borg?

00:28:51.140 --> 00:28:52.848
And I sort of look at
them gently and go,

00:28:52.848 --> 00:28:56.264
you have no idea of how much of
the internal Google ecosystem

00:28:56.264 --> 00:28:58.430
tentacles reach their way
inside our implementation.

00:28:58.430 --> 00:29:00.410
We couldn't do it
even if we wanted to.

00:29:00.410 --> 00:29:01.370
It's just too hard.

00:29:01.370 --> 00:29:04.180
But if you start from scratch,
and if you target being an open

00:29:04.180 --> 00:29:06.670
source project, and you
aim for an Apache license,

00:29:06.670 --> 00:29:08.750
and you use a different
language to make sure you

00:29:08.750 --> 00:29:10.940
don't accidentally have
dependencies on things--

00:29:10.940 --> 00:29:12.696
so Borg is in C++.

00:29:12.696 --> 00:29:16.210
Kubernetes is in Go-- you end
up with a thing that is actually

00:29:16.210 --> 00:29:19.025
far more malleable to the
outside world, which is great.

00:29:19.025 --> 00:29:20.400
I mean, that's
what it's all for.

00:29:20.400 --> 00:29:22.774
And, as a result, we have many
people contributing to it.

00:29:22.774 --> 00:29:24.700
And it's kind of
making rapid progress.

00:29:24.700 --> 00:29:26.450
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
one of the differences

00:29:26.450 --> 00:29:29.180
that I heard was also very
important-- other than open

00:29:29.180 --> 00:29:31.910
sourcing or not, was labels.

00:29:31.910 --> 00:29:35.200
I've heard that labels is a
thing that is not part of Borg.

00:29:35.200 --> 00:29:36.010
Is that true?

00:29:36.010 --> 00:29:39.369
And why do you think that
labels were added to Kubernetes?

00:29:39.369 --> 00:29:40.910
JOHN WILKES: Because
we actually were

00:29:40.910 --> 00:29:42.206
trying to add them for Borg.

00:29:42.206 --> 00:29:44.140
And we made progress on it.

00:29:44.140 --> 00:29:48.682
We used them in the outside
world, and our Google Cloud

00:29:48.682 --> 00:29:49.855
product e-platform.

00:29:49.855 --> 00:29:52.830
And they turn out to be
a much more flexible way

00:29:52.830 --> 00:29:54.110
of grouping things together.

00:29:54.110 --> 00:29:56.150
So Borg has a notion,
which we call a job.

00:29:56.150 --> 00:29:59.114
A job just a set of pretty
much identical tasks.

00:29:59.114 --> 00:30:00.030
But that's all it has.

00:30:00.030 --> 00:30:02.980
It doesn't have any way of
grouping subsets together,

00:30:02.980 --> 00:30:05.876
or overlapping subsets,
or those kind of things.

00:30:05.876 --> 00:30:07.000
And labels let you do this.

00:30:07.000 --> 00:30:10.370
You put a label, any label
you like, on a set of tasks

00:30:10.370 --> 00:30:14.160
and then say, do something like,
tell me the performance of it.

00:30:14.160 --> 00:30:16.350
Or do an upgrade
on, or take it down,

00:30:16.350 --> 00:30:19.960
or bring up a new version, or
et cetera on all the things

00:30:19.960 --> 00:30:21.970
for which this set
of labels applies.

00:30:21.970 --> 00:30:23.880
So now I have
basically Venn diagram

00:30:23.880 --> 00:30:26.049
set like behavior on any
mixture that you like.

00:30:26.049 --> 00:30:27.090
And you get to define it.

00:30:27.090 --> 00:30:29.350
You don't just have
this one single factor

00:30:29.350 --> 00:30:31.420
that is a very constrained
way of doing business.

00:30:31.420 --> 00:30:32.711
So that was one of the lessons.

00:30:32.711 --> 00:30:34.030
So Borg provided a primitive.

00:30:34.030 --> 00:30:36.700
It was helpful, but it
didn't go far enough.

00:30:36.700 --> 00:30:39.030
It was a simple new
abstraction of labels

00:30:39.030 --> 00:30:42.020
we get to take-- you can
reproduce the behavior we used

00:30:42.020 --> 00:30:44.320
to have in Borg, but you
also get all this other stuff

00:30:44.320 --> 00:30:44.860
as well.

00:30:44.860 --> 00:30:46.830
So that's a win-win.

00:30:46.830 --> 00:30:49.280
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
that actually makes,

00:30:49.280 --> 00:30:52.580
I guess, monitoring easier
because you can monitor per,

00:30:52.580 --> 00:30:55.540
really, the tasks
that you care about,

00:30:55.540 --> 00:30:57.800
which made me think
about, OK, so you

00:30:57.800 --> 00:31:00.140
said that everything
at Google runs

00:31:00.140 --> 00:31:02.420
on Borg-- or mostly everything.

00:31:02.420 --> 00:31:04.230
JOHN WILKES: Mostly everything.

00:31:04.230 --> 00:31:05.855
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And
that, to make sure

00:31:05.855 --> 00:31:08.470
that Borg is working
correctly, we have monitoring.

00:31:08.470 --> 00:31:12.505
Does the monitoring
for Borg run on Borg?

00:31:12.505 --> 00:31:13.630
JOHN WILKES: Good question.

00:31:13.630 --> 00:31:14.890
Yes, it does.

00:31:14.890 --> 00:31:18.170
So the monitoring systems
are jobs that Borg runs.

00:31:18.170 --> 00:31:21.100
But remember, I told you, if
the Borg master goes down,

00:31:21.100 --> 00:31:23.200
the systems it was
running stay up.

00:31:23.200 --> 00:31:25.210
There's no reason
why they should stop.

00:31:25.210 --> 00:31:28.600
And you can also monitor
one Borg cell from another.

00:31:28.600 --> 00:31:30.480
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Interesting.

00:31:30.480 --> 00:31:31.780
JOHN WILKES: So actually so
one of the interesting projects

00:31:31.780 --> 00:31:33.154
underway at the
moment is to say,

00:31:33.154 --> 00:31:36.138
what would happen if we
turned off all of Google

00:31:36.138 --> 00:31:38.510
and tried to boot
it up from scratch.

00:31:38.510 --> 00:31:39.520
MARK MANDEL: Oh god.

00:31:39.520 --> 00:31:40.660
JOHN WILKES: Because the
standard way of booting up

00:31:40.660 --> 00:31:43.130
a new cell is to go grab a
bunch of stuff from another one

00:31:43.130 --> 00:31:44.890
because it's up there.

00:31:44.890 --> 00:31:45.840
MARK MANDEL: But if you
don't have another other cell

00:31:45.840 --> 00:31:46.923
to go to, where do you go?

00:31:46.923 --> 00:31:48.230
JOHN WILKES: Yes, so exactly.

00:31:48.230 --> 00:31:50.730
So this is the kind of thinking
where somebody starts going,

00:31:50.730 --> 00:31:52.771
let's talk about failure
modes we haven't thought

00:31:52.771 --> 00:31:56.050
about before, which goes back
to one of our earlier topics.

00:31:56.050 --> 00:31:57.060
Imagine the worst case.

00:31:57.060 --> 00:31:58.310
No, no, the worst case.

00:31:58.310 --> 00:32:00.710
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Really
the worst case, wow.

00:32:00.710 --> 00:32:01.475
MARK MANDEL: That
almost comes back to,

00:32:01.475 --> 00:32:03.150
like, we were talking
about communities directly,

00:32:03.150 --> 00:32:05.691
like federated communities, and
being able to do cross cloud.

00:32:05.691 --> 00:32:07.920
And it's like, OK, yeah,
we have this cluster of 10,

00:32:07.920 --> 00:32:09.400
50, 100, 1,000 machines.

00:32:09.400 --> 00:32:10.010
That's great.

00:32:10.010 --> 00:32:11.695
What if that data
center goes down?

00:32:11.695 --> 00:32:13.620
Or what if that cloud
provider goes down?

00:32:13.620 --> 00:32:15.060
Or what if my
on-premise-- like, I

00:32:15.060 --> 00:32:16.920
want it to be in
multiple places at once.

00:32:16.920 --> 00:32:18.910
Let's think about that
as a failure scenario.

00:32:18.910 --> 00:32:21.555
JOHN WILKES: And it also gives
you freedom of deployment

00:32:21.555 --> 00:32:23.210
that separates the
decision about where

00:32:23.210 --> 00:32:25.860
you run from what your
application developers write.

00:32:25.860 --> 00:32:28.550
If you have a standard API,
like the Kubernetes one,

00:32:28.550 --> 00:32:29.850
you get to write to this API.

00:32:29.850 --> 00:32:32.387
And you can run exactly
the same thing locally.

00:32:32.387 --> 00:32:34.470
You can run it on premises
in your own data center

00:32:34.470 --> 00:32:34.970
if you want.

00:32:34.970 --> 00:32:36.650
You can run it on
cloud provider one,

00:32:36.650 --> 00:32:39.240
cloud provider two, or maybe
a mix of all of these things.

00:32:39.240 --> 00:32:41.444
And you can change
your mind at any time.

00:32:41.444 --> 00:32:43.110
You're not bound to
one of those things.

00:32:43.110 --> 00:32:45.050
So that freedom of
flexibility in deployment

00:32:45.050 --> 00:32:47.220
is a wonderful thing that
Kubernetes gives you.

00:32:47.220 --> 00:32:50.500
MARK MANDEL: Unfortunately,
we are running out of time.

00:32:50.500 --> 00:32:53.000
I'm dreadfully disappointed.

00:32:53.000 --> 00:32:55.310
Is there anything that
we haven't touched on,

00:32:55.310 --> 00:32:57.910
or you want to mention, or talk
about, or plug, or anything

00:32:57.910 --> 00:33:01.107
like that, that we
haven't had time to cover?

00:33:01.107 --> 00:33:02.940
JOHN WILKES: Google
Cloud is a wonderful way

00:33:02.940 --> 00:33:06.010
of being able to leverage
pretty much a bunch of stuff

00:33:06.010 --> 00:33:07.587
like the things I've
been describing.

00:33:07.587 --> 00:33:09.170
So you don't have
to do all that work.

00:33:09.170 --> 00:33:11.980
I mean, the same freedom that
we give internal developers is

00:33:11.980 --> 00:33:15.090
that part of the message behind
things like the cloud products.

00:33:15.090 --> 00:33:16.750
So you can rely on stuff.

00:33:16.750 --> 00:33:19.110
I was in Illinois
giving a talk last week

00:33:19.110 --> 00:33:22.450
about some of our data
flow and storage products.

00:33:22.450 --> 00:33:26.080
And in the middle of it, I was
able to do a live demo of doing

00:33:26.080 --> 00:33:29.050
a search in petabyte table.

00:33:29.050 --> 00:33:30.839
It took about 12 seconds.

00:33:30.839 --> 00:33:31.714
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Wow.

00:33:31.714 --> 00:33:32.560
MARK MANDEL: Wow.

00:33:32.560 --> 00:33:33.240
JOHN WILKES: That's
the kind of thing

00:33:33.240 --> 00:33:34.781
you want to be able
to take advantage

00:33:34.781 --> 00:33:36.370
of to get your job done.

00:33:36.370 --> 00:33:38.370
You shouldn't have to be
worrying about building

00:33:38.370 --> 00:33:39.400
the infrastructure to do that.

00:33:39.400 --> 00:33:40.280
So this is the
story behind Borg.

00:33:40.280 --> 00:33:41.640
It's the story behind
the Kubernetes.

00:33:41.640 --> 00:33:44.014
And it's the story behind the
entire Google cloud product

00:33:44.014 --> 00:33:44.600
line.

00:33:44.600 --> 00:33:47.340
Focus on what you can bring
and add value to, and then

00:33:47.340 --> 00:33:49.420
build on best in class
implementations of stuff

00:33:49.420 --> 00:33:51.842
underneath you can
get from other people.

00:33:51.842 --> 00:33:52.800
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Great.

00:33:52.800 --> 00:33:55.300
I have one more question.

00:33:55.300 --> 00:33:57.610
So you've been working on
Borg for quite a while.

00:33:57.610 --> 00:33:59.300
Can you share what
are you working on?

00:33:59.300 --> 00:34:01.700
Like, what is the
next crazy thing

00:34:01.700 --> 00:34:03.380
you're going to
be working about?

00:34:03.380 --> 00:34:10.650
If we interview you in a couple
