00:34:56.250 --> 00:34:58.870
So I think it's time to go
with the question of the week.

00:34:58.870 --> 00:35:01.369
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, I think this
is a really interesting one.

00:35:01.369 --> 00:35:06.320
So within the SDKs we
provide for Google Cloud

00:35:06.320 --> 00:35:08.830
for accessing services that
we have, like say, maybe data

00:35:08.830 --> 00:35:11.450
store, or authentication,
or storage,

00:35:11.450 --> 00:35:15.970
stuff like that, we have a thing
in there called application

00:35:15.970 --> 00:35:17.181
default credentials.

00:35:17.181 --> 00:35:18.430
And that makes it really easy.

00:35:18.430 --> 00:35:20.180
So if I've got, say,
some code, maybe it's

00:35:20.180 --> 00:35:24.050
running on an app engine, or
GCE, I can defautly just go,

00:35:24.050 --> 00:35:26.850
OK, I know that you're
running on GCE, which

00:35:26.850 --> 00:35:28.315
is internal to our
project, so you

00:35:28.315 --> 00:35:29.690
don't have to do
anything special

00:35:29.690 --> 00:35:32.110
to authenticate to log in to,
like, a particular service.

00:35:32.110 --> 00:35:32.900
And that's great.

00:35:32.900 --> 00:35:33.441
That's dandy.

00:35:33.441 --> 00:35:34.240
That's awesome.

00:35:34.240 --> 00:35:36.540
What do I do if I'm
testing locally,

00:35:36.540 --> 00:35:39.560
but I want to be able to connect
to that thing in the cloud

00:35:39.560 --> 00:35:40.890
that I would do normally?

00:35:40.890 --> 00:35:42.250
Is there an easy way?

00:35:42.250 --> 00:35:44.550
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yes, so
the important thing to know

00:35:44.550 --> 00:35:47.870
is that the way this
works is by accessing

00:35:47.870 --> 00:35:53.050
some URL that is only
accessible from inside

00:35:53.050 --> 00:35:54.570
of your own network.

00:35:54.570 --> 00:35:57.435
So if you're running it
locally, fortunately, you

00:35:57.435 --> 00:36:00.110
don't have access to
that because security.

00:36:00.110 --> 00:36:03.140
So the only way of
doing this before

00:36:03.140 --> 00:36:07.590
was to download a JSON file--
so go create a new service

00:36:07.590 --> 00:36:10.100
account on the console, and
then download that file,

00:36:10.100 --> 00:36:11.850
put it in a good place,
and then make sure

00:36:11.850 --> 00:36:16.210
that you have the correct global
variable, environment variable,

00:36:16.210 --> 00:36:18.240
pointing to that JSON file.

00:36:18.240 --> 00:36:19.160
MARK MANDEL: Ow.

00:36:19.160 --> 00:36:19.420
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:36:19.420 --> 00:36:20.500
MARK MANDEL: My brain now hurts.

00:36:20.500 --> 00:36:22.516
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Now,
you can do a gcloud beta

00:36:22.516 --> 00:36:25.160
of application default login.

00:36:25.160 --> 00:36:25.820
That's it.

00:36:25.820 --> 00:36:28.440
MARK MANDEL: Oh, and that
just grabs the thing it needs,

00:36:28.440 --> 00:36:30.877
does the magic, and
it suddenly works.

00:36:30.877 --> 00:36:32.752
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
it does all the magic.

00:36:32.752 --> 00:36:36.320
So basically, if you have a
call to get application default

00:36:36.320 --> 00:36:40.257
credential from your
code, it will just work.

00:36:40.257 --> 00:36:41.590
MARK MANDEL: That's really cool.

00:36:41.590 --> 00:36:42.570
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It
is pretty amazing.

00:36:42.570 --> 00:36:44.140
I've used it a couple times.

00:36:44.140 --> 00:36:47.620
And I was surprised
because I really expected,

00:36:47.620 --> 00:36:49.000
oh, so what is its next step.

00:36:49.000 --> 00:36:50.905
And its next step is your done.

00:36:50.905 --> 00:36:51.700
So, yay.

00:36:51.700 --> 00:36:52.280
Yeah.

00:36:52.280 --> 00:36:53.070
MARK MANDEL: So
that's really cool.

00:36:53.070 --> 00:36:54.860
It means, then, you can
be like sometimes maybe

00:36:54.860 --> 00:36:56.550
if you want to use an
emulator, if that's available,

00:36:56.550 --> 00:36:57.070
you can do that.

00:36:57.070 --> 00:36:58.340
Or you can connect
to the service that's

00:36:58.340 --> 00:36:59.660
actually in the
cloud, and you have

00:36:59.660 --> 00:37:01.050
really easy ways of doing both.

00:37:01.050 --> 00:37:02.130
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, as we mentioned

00:37:02.130 --> 00:37:04.290
in a previous episode,
there are some emulators,

00:37:04.290 --> 00:37:06.910
like data store has a emulator
for it, which is really cool.

00:37:06.910 --> 00:37:09.350
But some of the services do
not have those emulators.

00:37:09.350 --> 00:37:11.570
And sometimes you
don't want to emulate,

00:37:11.570 --> 00:37:14.470
you actually want to talk to
the service, the real service.

00:37:14.470 --> 00:37:17.370
And doing the authentication
in those cases

00:37:17.370 --> 00:37:18.930
can be a little bit hard.

00:37:18.930 --> 00:37:22.970
So yeah, that way you can
run your test directly

00:37:22.970 --> 00:37:26.420
on your laptop, which is on a
completely different network,

00:37:26.420 --> 00:37:30.950
without having any other
issues-- which is awesome.

00:37:30.950 --> 00:37:31.900
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

